  <revision rev="9" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>fea923a2b41f05e3efe77f0cb841ffa0</srcmd5>
    <version>64.8</version>
    <time>1786304683</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update to 64.8 (from 62.2). Supersedes SR 1370360, which I created with a placeholder message by accident; the tree is identical.

Upstream highlights: a new -turbo algorithm for the add command (explicitly experimental upstream), a new -recover extraction mode that reads the archive randomly and writes files sequentially - aimed at high-latency destinations and often recovering more from damaged archives, a rewritten dump command that handles archives of any size, SFTP bandwidth limiting via -bandwidth, new work fc / work crc32 / work ispasswordok commands, -stdinsize for accurate ETAs, dir -nodir, hash -norecursion and -always, better local time handling including negative UTC offsets, and a fix for deprecated streamed ZPAQ files being misdetected. The experimental FRANZEN encrypted format was added; upstream states it is not suitable for anything beyond experimentation.

CVE note: boo#1272902 / CVE-2025-50327 is open against this package but does not apply to a Linux build. The flaw is a failure to propagate the Windows Mark-of-the-Web to extracted files, and MotW is an NTFS Zone.Identifier alternate data stream that has no equivalent on Linux; the report also covers only 61.3 and earlier, while Factory has shipped 62.2 since May 2025. The changelog records this as a not-affected note.

NOTE FOR LEGAL REVIEW: License changes from 'MIT AND SUSE-Public-Domain' to 'Apache-2.0 AND BSD-2-Clause AND MIT AND SUSE-Public-Domain AND Zlib AND Unlicense AND Ferguson-Twofish'. This is a correction, not a new obligation - nothing new entered the source. zpaqfranz is a single translation unit embedding 25 third-party components, which upstream enumerates in the 'Credits and copyrights and licenses' block of zpaqfranz.cpp; the old tag named two of them. The per-component derivation is recorded in a Legal-Review-Notice comment above the tag. Two duals are elected: BLAKE3 is 'CC0-1.0 OR Apache-2.0' -&gt; Apache-2.0 (already required by HighwayHash), and the libtomcrypt AES is 'public domain OR WTFPL' -&gt; public domain. The bundled curl.h carries the curl licence but only the header is present - libcurl and libssh are dlopened at run time and neither is linked - so it is not in the tag.

Packaging cleanup: added a %check running upstream's own autotest plus an archive create/verify round trip (there was no %check at all); installed the man page upstream ships in man/; passed the distribution LDFLAGS, which were previously not passed at all so the binary got none of the standard link hardening; built with -DIPV6 so IPv4 is no longer forced; replaced 'Suggests: libcurl' (no such package exists in openSUSE) with Recommends on libcurl4 and libssh4, the libraries actually dlopened by name; declared the bundled blake3, highwayhash, libdivsufsort-lite, lz4 and xxhash copies with bundled() Provides; and normalised the CRLF line endings of CHANGELOG.md and COPYING.

The arch-specific compile flags are carried over unchanged and deliberately: x86_64 currently gets -DNOJIT and no -DHWSHA2 while i586 gets -DHWSHA2, which looks inverted and which Fedora handles differently, but I could not confirm that the SHA-NI code path is cpuid-gated and did not want to risk SIGILL on older hardware. Worth a follow-up by someone who can test it.

Built and verified on x86_64: rpmlint 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0 badness, %check autotest 'OK 16 ERROR 0' plus the round trip, source_validator clean.</comment>
    <requestid>1370361</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="10" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>470ab129e0ace39069641cb389bfff2f</srcmd5>
    <version>65.1</version>
    <time>1789993769</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to 65.1:
  * New mount command exposing an archive as a read-only FUSE
    filesystem (not enabled here: upstream calls it untested on
    Unix, and it would pull libfuse into the licence)
  * New xx command for sequential-write extraction
  * x -backupdir moves files aside instead of erasing them on
    overwrite
  * -franzen no longer writes a cleartext .zpaq
  * w -ramdisk extracts files larger than the chunk in sequential
    RAM windows, and its chunk counter no longer runs away
  * v no longer reports a file FAILED forever after a
    metadata-only change
  * t and pp -ssd pre-check the .franzen container

Upstream did not update CHANGELOG.md for this release; the bullets
come from the release notes. Licence unchanged, since the FUSE code
is not compiled. Upstream self-test passes.</comment>
    <requestid>1379320</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="11" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>70e76b067600d4b32011a3a27d56e6f2</srcmd5>
    <version>65.2</version>
    <time>1790107124</time>
    <user>dimstar_suse</user>
    <comment>Update to 65.2.

New zip command writing an archive out as one ZIP64 file, with -deflate
using a DEFLATE encoder written for the purpose rather than linking
zlib, -all writing one full-snapshot folder per version, and internal
expansion of quoted wildcards on Linux and BSD.

Carries a new downstream patch, zpaqfranz-qualify-std-string.patch,
because 65.2 does not compile with g++: it declares two new file-scope
globals as bare string at lines 4953 and 5117 while using std::string
does not appear until line 20673. Every neighbouring global in those
blocks is already qualified, so this reads as an oversight; MSVC
presumably accepts it through Windows header name-pulling. Reported
upstream as gh#fcorbelli/zpaqfranz#287 with the diff inline - their
repository is maintained by direct file uploads, so an issue rather
than a pull request.

The credited zip behaviour was exercised on the built RPM rather than
read from release notes: STORED ZIP64 verified with unzip -t, -deflate
compressing, -all producing VER00000000 and VER00000001 snapshot
folders with a pre-write space and memory estimate, quoted wildcards
expanding, and copy with duplicate sources counting each file once.
Windows-only items in the release - the mount rework, WinFsp, filename
sanitising and the rd changes - are deliberately not credited, since
this build defines neither ZPAQMOUNT nor _WIN32.

The licence position is unchanged: the credits block is byte-identical
to 65.1, the DEFLATE encoder is upstream's own code and no zlib is
linked, so the License tag, the Legal-Review-Notice and every
Provides: bundled() entry still hold.

Built and checked locally on aarch64: autotest OK 16, ERROR 0; rpmlint
0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 badness.</comment>
    <requestid>1379756</requestid>
  </revision>
